      pathophysiology: "Autoregulating arterioles dilate when perfusion pressure falls and constrict when it rises, buffering flow across a limited range. Inside an ischemic territory, those arterioles may already be maximally dilated, so flow becomes pressure-dependent: a further fall in mean arterial pressure can collapse collateral delivery. At the same time, cerebral edema raises intracranial pressure and narrows the perfusion gradient. This is why stroke teams avoid both untreated extreme hypertension that increases bleeding or edema risk and indiscriminate pressure reduction that can sacrifice penumbral flow; the safe target depends on stroke type, reperfusion treatment, vessel status, and protocol.",
