      definition: "The Circle of Willis is an arterial connection at the brain's base that links the two internal-carotid anterior circulations with the vertebrobasilar posterior circulation. Its communicating arteries can provide alternative routes when one inflow pathway narrows or closes. The downstream cerebral territories are clinically important because the middle, anterior, and posterior cerebral arteries nourish different motor, sensory, language, visual, memory, and behavioral networks, so the deficit pattern can help localize the threatened vessel before imaging confirms it.",
